[1mSTANDARDS[0m
     With the exception of options [1m-g[0m, [1m-n[0m and [1m-o[0m, the [1mls[0m utility conforms to IEEE Std 1003.1-2001
     (“POSIX.1”) and IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (“POSIX.1”).  The options [1m-B[0m, [1m-D[0m, [1m-G[0m, [1m-I[0m, [1m-T[0m, [1m-U[0m, [1m-W[0m,
     [1m-Z[0m, [1m-b[0m, [1m-h[0m, [1m-w[0m, [1m-y[0m and [1m-[0m, are non-standard extensions.

     The ACL support is compatible with IEEE Std 1003.2c (“POSIX.2c”) Draft 17 (withdrawn).

[1mHISTORY[0m
     An [1mls[0m command appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX.

[1mBUGS[0m
     To maintain backward compatibility, the relationships between the many options are quite
     complex.

     The exception mentioned in the [1m-s[0m option description might be a feature that was based on the
     fact that single-column output usually goes to something other than a terminal.  It is
     debatable whether this is a design bug.

     IEEE Std 1003.2 (“POSIX.2”) mandates opposite sort orders for files with the same timestamp
     when sorting with the [1m-t[0m option.

